# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values for local development.
# .env is gitignored and must never be committed — no secret belongs in source
# control, and no value in THIS file is a working credential.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# D-037: an application-owned environment variable is permitted ONLY when the
# value must be known before the database can be read, or when it determines
# where persistent state lives. Everything else is a database-backed setting.
# Adding a variable to this file requires an ADR.
#
# The list below is deliberately short. Session timeouts, the brand name, the
# default locale, the time zone, the maintenance banner — all of those are
# settings in the database (`Organization.config`), editable by an
# administrator at runtime, NOT environment variables.
#
# TZ, NODE_ENV, PORT, proxy settings, CA paths and container runtime settings
# are PLATFORM variables, not SplashTrack configuration, and are not listed as
# application config here.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- Where persistent state lives -------------------------------------------
# TWO connection strings, not one, and not three (D-182, ADR-0002).
#
# A connection to PostgreSQL carries a username, and that username decides what
# the connection may do. SplashTrack keeps an append-only audit trail — who read
# which child's medical note, who changed an exam result — and after a breach it
# is what answers "what happened, to whom, and when". Its value depends on being
# unfalsifiable. If the web application and the audit trail shared a username,
# anything that hijacked the web application would inherit the ability to erase
# the record of doing so: the log guarded by the same key as the door it watches.
#
# Neither has a default. A missing or wrong value must fail loudly at boot
# rather than quietly reach the wrong database. `docker compose up postgres`
# starts a local Postgres matching docker-compose.yml, and creates both roles.

# The RUNTIME role. What the web process connects as, and therefore what an SQL
# injection or a leaked copy of this file yields. It owns nothing, is not a
# superuser, and on `AuditEvent` it holds INSERT and SELECT and nothing else —
# which is what makes it D-149's append-only writer. There is no separate writer
# connection; ADR-0002 §7.5 says why a second pool in the same Node process
# would be a diagram rather than a boundary. Shared with the Prisma runtime
# client (src/lib/database/client.ts).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://splashtrack_app:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/splashtrack

# The MAINTENANCE role — ADR-0002 §5's "the part only the operator can start".
# It carries `splashtrack_retention`, which holds the ONLY `DELETE` on
# `AuditEvent` (D-168's checkpointed prune is the only legitimate deleter) and
# is a member of the non-connecting `splashtrack_owner`.
#
# `prisma migrate deploy` runs through it too, and the Prisma CLI reads it via
# prisma.config.ts. The MIGRATION connection is not a third variable: it is this
# one plus `options=-c role=splashtrack_owner`, derived in
# src/lib/database/role-model.ts, so every table is owned by a role that no
# connection string anywhere can name. That is the precondition D-149 part 2
# never stated, and without which its REVOKE is decorative — an owner re-grants
# itself in one statement (ADR-0002 §3).
#
# WHY IT IS A SECOND VARIABLE AND NOT DERIVED FROM THE FIRST — the D-037 gate:
# it carries a different username AND a different password. A scheme that
# derived it would put the maintenance role one string manipulation away from
# the runtime role, which is the whole separation. ADR-0002 §8 is the ADR D-037
# requires for it.
DATABASE_MAINTENANCE_URL=postgresql://splashtrack_retention:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/splashtrack

# --- Must be known before the database can be read --------------------------
# THE ONE BOOTSTRAP SECRET (D-112). A path to a file holding 32 random bytes,
# base64-encoded. It is the root of EVERY application key: the Better Auth
# signing secret, TOTP secret encryption, settings-registry secrets, encrypted
# columns, the backup master key and the audit checkpoint MAC are all
# HKDF-SHA256 derivations of it under a purpose label. See
# `src/lib/crypto/secret-key.ts` and design 13 §3.1.1.
#
# A FILE, not a value: an environment variable is readable via `docker inspect`,
# /proc/<pid>/environ, crash dumps and — most commonly — the operator's own
# compose file, committed. The application NEVER generates this key; with
# neither variable set it refuses to start.
#
# Generate with: npm run secret:init -- ./secrets/secret_key
SECRET_KEY_FILE=./secrets/secret_key

# DEPRECATED fallback — the secret inline instead of in a file. Accepted so an
# install supplying it is not bricked, and it reports itself so the diagnostics
# page can warn. Prefer SECRET_KEY_FILE.
# SECRET_KEY=

# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET IS GONE, and must not come back. It was a SECOND
# independent secret beside this one — exactly the shape F-95 describes: a
# restore that reproduces one but not the other leaves every TOTP enrolment
# silently dead, while MFA is mandatory for administrators. The signing secret
# is now HKDF(SECRET_KEY, info="auth-signing-v1"), so a restore reproduces it.

# Public base URL of this instance (scheme + host + optional port, no trailing
# slash). Better Auth derives cookie/redirect origins from it, and the WebAuthn
# relying-party id is derived from its host — which is why it cannot be a
# runtime setting: it is read once at auth-context construction.
#
# WARNING (D-132, FM-15): changing the host here changes the WebAuthn RP ID and
# INVALIDATES EVERY EXISTING PASSKEY at once. Moving from a LAN name to a real
# domain is the expected path for this deployment, so set it deliberately.
# Every account keeps a password + TOTP fallback precisely so this is
# recoverable rather than a total lockout.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional: pin the WebAuthn relying-party id explicitly instead of deriving it
# from BETTER_AUTH_URL's host. Use the registrable parent domain when the app is
# served from a subdomain and passkeys should work on both. Same "changing this
# invalidates every passkey" warning applies.
# PASSKEY_RP_ID=

# --- Test harness (optional) ------------------------------------------------
# The vitest suite derives its database from DATABASE_URL by appending `_test`
# to the database name, and refuses to run against anything else. Set this only
# when the test database lives somewhere else entirely (e.g. a CI service
# container). It is subject to the same `_test`-suffix guard.
#
# CREATEDB LIVES ON THE MAINTENANCE ROLE, NOT ON THIS ONE. The harness creates
# its own `_test` database and `tests/integration/boot-state-matrix.test.ts`
# drops and creates a throwaway one per case, but it does all of that through
# DATABASE_MAINTENANCE_URL. The runtime role in a checkout is therefore shaped
# EXACTLY like the runtime role in production — which is the point: a missing
# grant on a table a new migration created should fail on a laptop, not for the
# first time in production (ADR-0002 §4).
#
# CREATEDB is NOT SUPERUSER, and the phase 1.0 report's claim that a
# least-privilege role would break the harness conflated the two. The full suite
# passes against `NOSUPERUSER NOCREATEROLE NOBYPASSRLS CREATEDB` and fails with
# `permission denied to create database` without it — measured both directions
# in `docs/adr/0002-database-roles-and-least-privilege.md` §6.
#
# Set this to a `_test` database reachable by the RUNTIME role. Its maintenance
# counterpart is derived from DATABASE_MAINTENANCE_URL the same way.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Compose variables (NOT SplashTrack application configuration) ----------
# Read by `docker-compose.yml`, never by the application. They are listed here
# because Compose reads this same `.env` file.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD has no default, for the same reason DATABASE_URL has none.
# It previously defaulted to the literal string `splashtrack`, justified in the
# compose file as "safe on a laptop" — while that same file published 5432 on
# 0.0.0.0. Docker publishes through the nat table and the FORWARD chain, so a
# published port does NOT pass the INPUT rules where ufw lives: the pair was a
# SUPERUSER database open to the internet under a guessable password. The port
# is loopback-only now and this value is required; either alone would have
# prevented it.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is now the PROVISIONING SUPERUSER's password (D-182). It no
# longer appears in DATABASE_URL and is not an application credential: its only
# job is to run `infra/provision-roles.sql` when the volume is first
# initialised. Generate one:
#     openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The two role passwords `infra/postgres-init/10-provision-roles.sh` creates the
# roles with. They must match the passwords inside DATABASE_URL and
# DATABASE_MAINTENANCE_URL above.
#
# The duplication is real and is stated rather than hidden: the Postgres image
# needs the password as a VALUE to create a role with, and the application needs
# it inside a URL to connect with. Neither could safely have a default, and
# deriving one from the other would defeat the separation.
#
# GENERATE THEM WITH `openssl rand -hex 24`, NOT `-base64`.
#
#     openssl rand -hex 24        (twice — they must not be the same password)
#
# base64 output contains `/` and `+`, and a `/` in a password ends the authority
# section of a URL: `postgresql://user:a/b@host:5432/db` does not parse, and
# every command fails at once with nothing pointing at the password. Percent-
# encoding works too (`/` → %2F, `@` → %40, `+` → %2B) but hex avoids the whole
# question. This bit the first run of this very change.
SPLASHTRACK_APP_PASSWORD=
SPLASHTRACK_RETENTION_PASSWORD=

# CREATEDB on the retention role. `off` for any real deployment: production
# never creates a database — the entrypoint runs `migrate deploy`, never
# `CREATE DATABASE`. Set it to `on` in a CHECKOUT, where `npm test` builds its
# own `_test` and throwaway databases (ADR-0002 §6).
# SPLASHTRACK_PROVISION_CREATEDB=off

# Which HOST port maps to the container's 3000. Must match BETTER_AUTH_URL.
# APP_PORT=3000

# --- Platform variables (NOT SplashTrack application configuration) ---------
# These configure the process, not the swim school. They carry no domain
# meaning and are exactly what a container platform expects to set. See
# `docs/adr/0001-log-level-is-an-environment-variable.md`.
#
# LOG_LEVEL is here rather than in the settings registry because the logger is
# constructed BEFORE any database connection is opened — it is the thing that
# reports why a connection failed, and it logs the boot state machine's AHEAD
# and FAILED states, in which the database is unusable by definition. A level
# fetched from the database would be unavailable in every situation where an
# operator most needs to raise it.
#
# Defaults to `info` in production and `debug` otherwise. Changing it needs a
# restart; that is accepted (see the ADR).
# LOG_LEVEL=info
